NNAAMMEE
     ssuu — substitute user identity

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssuu [--ffKKLLllmm] [--aa _a_u_t_h_-_t_y_p_e] [--cc _l_o_g_i_n_-_c_l_a_s_s] [--ss _l_o_g_i_n_-_s_h_e_l_l]
        [_l_o_g_i_n [_s_h_e_l_l_-_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t _._._.]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssuu utility allows a user to run a shell with the user and group ID of
     another user without having to log out and in as that other user.  All of
     the real, effective, and saved user and group IDs as well as all supple‐
     mentary group IDs are always set according to the target user.  If the
     target _l_o_g_i_n name is not specified, “root” is used.

     By default, the shell of the target login is invoked and the SHELL and
     HOME environment variables are set according to the target login, whereas
     the current working directory remains unchanged.  If the target login has
     a user ID of 0, LOGNAME and USER are preserved and PATH and the umask(2)
     value are set according to login.conf(5); otherwise, LOGNAME and USER are
     set to the target login and PATH and the umask(2) value are preserved.
     The TERM environment variable is always preserved.  The rest of the envi‐
     ronment remains unmodified by default.

     The options are as follows:

     --       Same as the --ll option (deprecated).

     --aa _a_u_t_h_-_t_y_p_e
             Specify an authentication type such as “skey” or “radius”.

     --cc _l_o_g_i_n_-_c_l_a_s_s
             Specify a login class.  You may only override the default class
             if you're already root.

     --ff      If the invoked shell is csh(1), this option prevents it from exe‐
             cuting system or user startup files.  For other shells, start a
             regular shell instead of a login shell when the --ll option is
             used.  Useful to skip reading shell initialization files.

     --KK      This is shorthand for “ssuu --aa _p_a_s_s_w_d”, provided for backwards com‐
             patibility.

     --LL      Loop until a correct username and password combination is en‐
             tered, similar to login(1).  Note that in this mode target _l_o_g_i_n
             must be specified explicitly, either on the command line or in‐
             teractively.  Additionally, ssuu will prompt for the password even
             when invoked by root.

     --ll      Simulate a full login.  The shell of the target login is invoked
             and the current working directory is changed to the home direc‐
             tory of the target login.  HOME, SHELL, LOGNAME, and USER are set
             to the default values for the target login.  PATH and the
             umask(2) value are set according to login.conf(5).  Except for
             preserving TERM, the rest of the environment is discarded.

     --mm      Leave the environment unmodified.  The login shell of the invok‐
             ing user is started, and the current working directory is not
             changed.  As a security precaution, if the target user's shell is
             a non-standard shell (as defined by getusershell(3)) and the
             caller's real UID is non-zero, ssuu will fail.

     --ss _l_o_g_i_n_-_s_h_e_l_l
             Specify the path to an alternate login shell.  You may only over‐
             ride the shell if you're already root.  This option will override
             the shell even if the --mm option is specified.

     The --ll and --mm options are mutually exclusive; the last one specified
     overrides any previous ones.

     If shell arguments are provided on the command line, they are passed to
     the login shell of the target login.  This allows it to pass arbitrary
     commands via the --cc option as understood by most shells.  Note that --cc
     usually expects a single argument only; you have to quote it when passing
     multiple words.

     If group 0 (normally “wheel”) has users listed then only those users can
     ssuu to “root”.  It is not sufficient to change a user's _/_e_t_c_/_p_a_s_s_w_d entry
     to add them to the “wheel” group; they must explicitly be listed in
     _/_e_t_c_/_g_r_o_u_p.  If no one is in the “wheel” group, it is ignored, and anyone
     who knows the root password is permitted to ssuu to “root”.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The following list provides the values of environment variables in the
     new shell that is started by ssuu.

     HOME     The home directory of the target login, except that it remains
              unchanged with --mm.

     LOGNAME  The target login by default, but unchanged if the target login
              has a UID of 0 or if --mm is given.

     PATH     The search path.  It remains unchanged by default, but is set
              according to the target login if the target login has a UID of 0
              or if --ll is given.

     PWD      The current working directory.  It remains unchanged by default,
              but is set to the home directory of the target login with --ll.

     SHELL    The new shell that is started.  It is the shell of the target
              login by default, but the shell of the invoking user with --mm.

     TERM     The terminal type.  It is always retained from the invoking
              process.

     USER     Same as LOGNAME.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Run the command “makewhatis” as user “bin”.  You will be asked for bin's
     password unless your real UID is 0.

           $ su bin -c makewhatis

     Same as above, but the target command consists of more than a single
     word:

           $ su bin -c 'makewhatis /usr/local/man'

     Same as above, but the target command is run with the resource limits of
     the login class “staff”.  Note that the first --cc option applies to ssuu
     while the second is an argument to the shell.

           $ su -c staff bin -c 'makewhatis /usr/local/man'

     Pretend a login for user “foo”:

           $ su -l foo

     Same as above, but use S/Key for authentication:

           $ su -a skey -l foo

